"""Chapter 3: Preliminaries"""
import os

FIG = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "fig")


def build(r):
    r.chapter("Preliminaries")
    r.p("This chapter presents the fundamental concepts, technologies and techniques that are needed to understand the design and implementation of the AI-Powered Intelligent Recruitment System. It covers the programming ecosystem, the extraction of text from resumes, the vector representations used to compare documents (TF-IDF, BM25 and dense sentence embeddings), the skill ontology with fuzzy matching, the notions of explainability and fairness that guide the design, multi-agent deliberation, and the metrics used to evaluate the system. The mathematical definitions given here are used again in Chapter 6, where their implementation is described.")

    r.h1("3.1", "Python and Its Ecosystem")
    r.p("Python is a high-level, interpreted, general-purpose programming language that is widely used for text processing, data analysis and machine learning. In this project Python implements the entire scoring core: parsing, ontology matching, ranking, the council, the GitHub inspector, the talent search and the interview generator. Its strengths for this work are a readable syntax, a rich standard library (regular expressions, sequence matching, dataclasses and HTTP clients), and a mature scientific ecosystem.")
    r.p("The libraries that matter most are scikit-learn [@sklearn], which provides the TF-IDF vectoriser and the cosine-similarity function; NumPy and pandas [@pandas], which hold the score arrays and tabular analytics; the Hugging Face Transformers ecosystem [@hftransformers], from which the sentence-embedding model is loaded when it is installed; PyMuPDF and python-docx, which extract text from PDF and DOCX files; and pytesseract, a wrapper around the Tesseract optical character recognition engine [@tesseract]. Matplotlib [@matplotlib] was used to draw the charts of the evaluation chapter. The modular nature of Python allows the parsing, scoring and decision logic to be written as independent classes that can be tested one by one.")
    r.p("The recruiter-facing parts rely on web technologies. A REST (Representational State Transfer) service exposes resources such as jobs, resumes and screenings through uniform HTTP operations [@fielding]; FastAPI implements this service in Python, and the React library renders the browser interface. The advantages of Python for the proposed system include:", keep=True)
    r.bullets([
        "simple and readable syntax that shortens development and review;",
        "extensive libraries for text processing, retrieval and machine learning;",
        "support for modular, testable code with the pytest framework;",
        "easy integration with web frameworks and relational databases; and",
        "portability across Windows and Linux environments.",
    ])

    r.h1("3.2", "Resume Parsing and Text Extraction")
    r.p("A resume is an unstructured document. Before any scoring is possible, the text must be extracted and organised into fields. Documents in PDF format either contain a text layer, which can be read directly, or consist of scanned page images, which must be converted to text by optical character recognition. Tesseract [@tesseract] recognises characters in an image in several stages: it finds text lines and words, recognises each word in two passes with an adaptive classifier, and applies linguistic analysis to the result. In the proposed system a page is sent to OCR only when its text layer contains almost no text, which keeps the common case fast.")
    r.p("After extraction, the text is divided into sections (skills, education, experience, projects, certifications) by recognising header patterns such as *Technical Skills* or *Work Experience*. Contact details are found with regular expressions. Employment dates are collected as intervals with a resolution of one month. If a date is written as a month and a year, it is converted to a month index m = 12y + mon, so that durations and overlaps become simple integer arithmetic. Total experience is the length of the union of all valid intervals, so that two overlapping roles are not counted twice:")
    r.eq(r"E=\frac{1}{12}\,\left|\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\,[\,s_i,\;e_i\,]\right|")
    r.where([("E", "total years of experience;"), ("s~i~, e~i~", "start and end month indices of the i-th valid role;"), ("n", "number of roles with a valid interval.")])
    r.p("An interval with e~i~ < s~i~, or one that starts in the future, is not valid. It is excluded from the experience total and reported as an inconsistency, because it signals either an error or an attempt to mislead. This simple temporal reasoning is invisible to text-similarity methods, which treat a date as just another token.")

    r.h1("3.3", "Vector Space Model and TF-IDF")
    r.p("The vector space model represents a document as a vector in which every dimension corresponds to a term. The importance of a term in a document is estimated by TF-IDF (term frequency, inverse document frequency) weighting [@tfidf]. The idea, introduced as term specificity [@jones], is that a term which occurs often in a document is important for it, but a term that occurs in almost every document is a poor discriminator and should be down-weighted. The weight of a term t in a document d is:")
    r.eq(r"w(t,d)=\mathrm{tf}(t,d)\cdot\mathrm{idf}(t)")
    r.eq(r"\mathrm{idf}(t)=\ln\frac{1+N}{1+\mathrm{df}(t)}+1")
    r.where([("tf(t, d)", "number of occurrences of term t in document d;"), ("df(t)", "number of documents that contain t;"), ("N", "total number of documents.")])
    r.p("Equation (3.3) is the smoothed form of idf used by scikit-learn [@sklearn], where the added constants avoid division by zero. The vectoriser used in this project removes English stop words, uses unigrams and bigrams, and keeps at most 3000 features. Two documents are compared by the cosine of the angle between their vectors:")
    r.eq(r"\cos(\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v})=\frac{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}}{\Vert\mathbf{u}\Vert\,\Vert\mathbf{v}\Vert}")
    r.p("Cosine similarity ignores the length of the documents and takes values between 0 and 1 for non-negative TF-IDF vectors. TF-IDF is fast, needs no training and is fully reproducible, which is why it is the offline fallback for the semantic component of the ranker. Its weakness is that it is purely lexical: the sentences *built REST services* and *developed HTTP APIs* share no word, so their TF-IDF similarity is close to zero although their meaning is the same [@manning].")

    r.h1("3.4", "Dense Sentence Embeddings")
    r.p("Dense embeddings map text to vectors of a few hundred real numbers such that texts with similar meaning lie close together. Early word embeddings such as word2vec [@word2vec] and GloVe [@glove] gave every word one vector. Transformer models [@transformer] produce context-dependent representations, and BERT [@bert] and its refinements [@roberta] became the standard encoders for language understanding. A plain BERT model compares two texts by feeding both through the network at once, which is accurate but slow when many pairs must be compared. Sentence-BERT [@sbert] removes this cost by encoding each text once into a fixed-size vector, so that comparison reduces to a cosine similarity, Equation (3.4). The compact model all-MiniLM-L6-v2 used in this project is a distilled transformer [@minilm] that produces vectors with 384 dimensions and runs comfortably on a laptop CPU.")
    r.p("When the sentence-transformers library is installed the semantic score of a candidate is the cosine similarity between the embedding of the resume and the embedding of the job description. When it is not installed, the TF-IDF cosine is used instead. The interface reports which backend is active so that the recruiter always knows how the semantic part of a score was obtained.")
    r.p("Dense models also have drawbacks that matter in hiring. They can over-match generic text, they are harder to explain than lexical scores, and they inherit social bias from their training data: the associations learnt by word embeddings reproduce human-like stereotypes [@caliskan,@bolukbasi], and recent audits of embedding-based resume retrieval found strong preferences related to the name of the candidate [@wilson]. For this reason the semantic similarity carries only 20 percent of the overall score in the proposed ranker, and the system removes the name before any text is embedded when blind screening is on. Cross-encoder re-rankers [@nogueira,@linrank] are more accurate than bi-encoders for fine ranking, but they need a pair-wise pass for every candidate and are listed as future work.")

    r.h1("3.5", "Okapi BM25 and Hybrid Retrieval")
    r.p("BM25 is a probabilistic ranking function that improves on TF-IDF by saturating the effect of term frequency and by normalising for document length [@bm25]. For a query q and a document d it is defined as:")
    r.eq(r"\mathrm{BM25}(q,d)=\sum_{t\in q}\mathrm{idf}_{\mathrm{BM}}(t)\cdot\frac{f(t,d)\,(k_1+1)}{f(t,d)+k_1\left(1-b+b\,\dfrac{|d|}{\mathrm{avgdl}}\right)}")
    r.eq(r"\mathrm{idf}_{\mathrm{BM}}(t)=\ln\left(1+\frac{N-n_t+0.5}{n_t+0.5}\right)")
    r.where([("f(t, d)", "frequency of term t in document d;"), ("|d|, avgdl", "length of d and average document length in the collection;"), ("k~1~, b", "tuning parameters; this project uses k~1~ = 1.5 and b = 0.75;"), ("n~t~", "number of documents that contain t.")])
    r.p("Dense retrievers outperform sparse ones when the query and the document use different words for the same idea. In one study, dense passage retrieval beat a strong BM25 system by 9 to 19 percentage points in top-20 accuracy [@dpr]. Sparse methods remain better at rare, exact terms such as the name of a tool, for example *Kubernetes* or *PyTorch*. Because a recruiter query often mixes concepts and tool names, the proposed talent search fuses both kinds of evidence at the level of a resume chunk:")
    r.eq(r"s(q,c)=\alpha\,s_{\mathrm{dense}}(q,c)+(1-\alpha)\,s_{\mathrm{BM25}}(q,c),\qquad \alpha=0.55")
    r.p("Here c is a chunk of a resume (a section or part of a section), and both scores are normalised to the range 0 to 1 before they are combined. An alternative way of fusing rankings, reciprocal rank fusion, adds 1/(k + rank) over the lists to be combined [@rrf]; score fusion was preferred here because both scores are available and calibrated per chunk. Retrieval-augmented systems ground their answers in retrieved text [@rag], and the recruiter chatbot of this project follows the same principle: it composes answers from retrieved and scored resume evidence and not from free text generation. Figure 3.1 shows the flow of a talent-search query.", keep=True)
    r.fig(os.path.join(FIG, "fig_3_1_retrieval.png"), "Hybrid retrieval flow of the talent search: query understanding, dense and BM25 scoring, fusion and per-candidate aggregation.", 14.0)
    r.table(["Property", "TF-IDF / BM25 (sparse)", "Sentence embeddings (dense)"], [
        ["Matches", "Exact and stemmed terms", "Meaning, paraphrases"],
        ["Rare tool names", "Strong", "Can be weak"],
        ["Paraphrase", "Weak", "Strong"],
        ["Training", "None", "Pre-trained model needed"],
        ["Explainability", "High, term by term", "Low"],
        ["Bias risk from training data", "Low", "Present [@wilson,@caliskan]"],
        ["Offline use", "Always", "Needs the model files"],
    ], "Comparison of Sparse and Dense Representations", [1.2, 1.7, 1.7], size=10.5, align=["left", "left", "left"])

    r.h1("3.6", "Skill Ontology, Fuzzy Matching and Partial Credit")
    r.p("A skill ontology is a controlled vocabulary in which every skill has one canonical name and a set of aliases. Aliases capture the many ways in which the same skill is written, for example *sklearn*, *scikit learn* and *Scikit-Learn*, or *k8s* and *Kubernetes*. Normalising every skill mentioned in a resume or a job description to its canonical name is a prerequisite for comparing them. Large taxonomies such as ESCO have thousands of skills and can be matched with language models [@clavie,@bhola], but such matching is probabilistic. The ontology of this project is deliberately small (79 skills) and every credit given by it can be traced to an explicit rule.")
    r.p("The normalisation of a skill string proceeds in three steps: an exact alias lookup, a lookup after stripping punctuation, and finally a fuzzy match against all aliases. The fuzzy step uses the similarity ratio of the sequence matcher of Python's standard library, which is based on the longest common contiguous blocks of the two strings (the Ratcliff-Obershelp idea):")
    r.eq(r"\mathrm{ratio}(a,b)=\frac{2M}{|a|+|b|}")
    r.where([("M", "number of characters in the matching blocks of the strings a and b;"), ("|a|, |b|", "lengths of the two strings.")])
    r.p("A string is mapped to an alias only when the ratio exceeds 0.88, so that a small spelling error is tolerated but unrelated skills are not merged. Edit distance in the sense of Levenshtein [@levenshtein] counts the minimum number of insertions, deletions and substitutions needed to turn one string into another; the sequence-matching ratio used here is a related, similarity-based measure that is faster to compute for short strings.")
    r.p("After normalisation every required skill is classified as *matched* if the candidate has the same canonical skill, *partially matched* if the candidate has a skill that is related to it through an explicit table of fifteen pairs (for example FastAPI and Flask, Docker and Kubernetes, or Machine Learning and Deep Learning), and *missing* otherwise. A matched skill earns full credit and a partially matched skill earns half credit. The skill coverage of a candidate is therefore:")
    r.eq(r"C_{\mathrm{skill}}=\frac{n_m+0.5\,n_p}{n_r}")
    r.where([("n~m~", "number of matched required skills;"), ("n~p~", "number of partially matched required skills;"), ("n~r~", "total number of required skills.")])
    r.p("A skill that is claimed is not necessarily a skill that is possessed. To reflect this, the proposed system distinguishes the required skills that appear anywhere in the resume (set L) from those that also appear outside the skills block, in experience or project text (set E). Evidence-weighted coverage gives half credit to a skill that is only listed:")
    r.eq(r"c_{\mathrm{ev}}=\frac{|E|+0.5\,\bigl(|L|-|E|\bigr)}{|R|}")
    r.where([("R", "set of required skills of the job;"), ("L ⊆ R", "required skills found anywhere in the resume;"), ("E ⊆ L", "required skills that are also evidenced in experience or project text.")])
    r.p("The ratio of listed skills that have no supporting evidence, (|L| − |E|)/|L|, is used to detect keyword stuffing. Figure 3.2 summarises the decision flow of the skill matcher.", keep=True)
    r.fig(os.path.join(FIG, "fig_3_2_skillmatch.png"), "Decision flow of the skill matcher: normalisation, exact match, related-pair match and missing skill.", 12.5)

    r.h1("3.7", "Explainability and Fairness")
    r.h2("3.7.1", "Explainable AI")
    r.p("Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is concerned with making the output of an automated system understandable to the people who use it or are affected by it [@arrieta]. Research in the social sciences shows that people expect explanations to be contrastive, selective and tied to causes that they can act upon [@miller]. Methods for explaining a black-box model after the fact, such as LIME [@lime] and SHAP [@shap], approximate the behaviour of a complex model around one prediction and are surveyed in [@guidotti]. A different position holds that for high-stakes decisions an interpretable model should be used from the start instead of explaining a black box [@rudin], and that the quality of an explanation has to be evaluated rigorously [@doshivelez]. The proposed system follows the second position: it uses transparent rules and weighted sums whose parts can be shown to the user, and it produces explanations (reasons, strengths, warnings, skill gaps and red flags) as part of the computation instead of reconstructing them afterwards.")
    r.h2("3.7.2", "Fairness")
    r.p("Fairness in algorithmic decision-making has several, partly conflicting definitions. Individual fairness requires that similar individuals are treated similarly [@dwork]. Group criteria such as equality of opportunity require equal error rates across protected groups [@hardt], and it is known that several natural group criteria cannot all hold at once when base rates differ [@kleinberg]. Surveys list many sources of bias, from the data to the model to the way results are used [@mehrabi]. For rankings, FA*IR tests whether a protected group is sufficiently represented in every prefix of a top-k list [@fair]. Applied to hiring, the literature shows that fairness cannot be reduced to a statistical test, and that legal notions of discrimination, vendor practice and technical metrics differ [@sanchez,@raghavan].")
    r.p("The proposed system uses three complementary measures. First, blind screening removes direct identifiers (name, e-mail, phone, location, profile links and gendered words) before any agent evaluates the resume. Second, a Fair-Hiring Auditor agent computes a merit-only score from job-related evidence and can reduce the consensus weight of an agent whose reasoning shows bias. Third, a counterfactual name-swap test checks that the score does not change when only the name is replaced, following the design of the classical audit studies [@bertrand]. The invariance is measured as:")
    r.eq(r"\Delta_{\mathrm{score}}=\max_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\,\bigl|\,S(x_n)-S(x)\,\bigr|")
    r.where([("x", "original resume;"), ("x~n~", "the same resume with the name replaced by n in the name field and in the text;"), ("𝒩", "set of substituted names;"), ("S(·)", "score or council consensus of a resume.")])
    r.p("Good practice in documentation, such as model cards [@modelcards] and datasheets for datasets [@datasheets], and internal algorithmic auditing [@raji], recommends that the intended use, the data and the limitations of a system are recorded. Chapter 1 and Chapter 8 of this report state the intended use, the synthetic nature of the data and the limitations in this spirit. Finally, the regulatory background includes the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, which treats recruitment systems as high-risk [@euaiact], and the General Data Protection Regulation, which governs the processing of the personal data found in resumes [@gdpr].")

    r.h1("3.8", "Multi-Agent Deliberation")
    r.p("A multi-agent system divides a complex judgement among several specialised components, each of which examines the problem from one point of view. With language models, it has been shown that letting several model instances propose answers and then criticise each other improves factuality and reasoning [@debate,@liang_mad], that teams of referees judge text closer to human annotators than a single referee [@chateval], and that agent frameworks can coordinate several roles to solve tasks [@autogen,@metagpt]. Two further ideas are often combined with such systems: interleaving reasoning with actions [@react] and sampling several reasoning paths and taking the consistent answer [@selfconsistency]. A caution comes from the study of language models used as judges, which can be biased by the position of an answer or by its length [@mtbench].")
    r.p("The council of this project borrows the structure of these systems, which is independent evaluation followed by cross-examination and synthesis, but not their non-determinism. Each agent is a transparent rule-based evaluator that can run offline. An optional language-model service may enrich the text of an agent and may move its score by at most 10 points, but the final verdict is always computed locally, so that it remains auditable. A fourth agent, the Fair-Hiring Auditor, reviews the other three. This design keeps the benefit of multiple viewpoints without making the decision depend on a generative model.")

    r.h1("3.9", "Evaluation Metrics")
    r.p("The agreement between two rankings of the same candidates, for example the ranking of the keyword baseline and the ranking of the proposed ranker, is measured with Kendall's rank-correlation coefficient [@kendall]:")
    r.eq(r"\tau=\frac{n_c-n_d}{\binom{n}{2}}")
    r.where([("n~c~", "number of concordant pairs, which are ordered the same way in both rankings;"), ("n~d~", "number of discordant pairs;"), ("n", "number of ranked items.")])
    r.p("A value of 1 means identical rankings and -1 means reversed rankings. To test whether the conclusions depend on the expert-chosen weights, each weight is multiplied by a random factor and renormalised, and the resulting ranking is compared with the default ranking:")
    r.eq(r"\tilde{w}_i=\frac{w_i\,u_i}{\sum_{k}w_k\,u_k},\qquad u_i\sim\mathcal{U}[0.8,\,1.2]")
    r.p("The quality of a search result list is measured with precision and recall at k, and with the normalised discounted cumulative gain [@ndcg]:")
    r.eq(r"\mathrm{P@}k=\frac{|\mathrm{Rel}\cap\mathrm{Top}_k|}{k},\qquad \mathrm{R@}k=\frac{|\mathrm{Rel}\cap\mathrm{Top}_k|}{|\mathrm{Rel}|}")
    r.eq(r"\mathrm{nDCG@}k=\frac{\mathrm{DCG@}k}{\mathrm{IDCG@}k},\qquad \mathrm{DCG@}k=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{2^{\,rel_i}-1}{\log_2(i+1)}")
    r.where([("Rel", "set of relevant items;"), ("Top~k~", "the first k items returned;"), ("rel~i~", "graded relevance of the item at position i;"), ("IDCG@k", "DCG@k of the ideal ordering.")])
    r.p("The agreement between the verdict of the system and a reference label is shown in a confusion matrix, whose entry in row i and column j counts the items of reference class i that received verdict j. Processing time is reported per resume and per stage. All of these metrics are used in Chapters 7 and 8. As Manning, Raghavan and Schütze point out, the choice of metric must follow the use of the system [@manning], which is why a ranking metric, a name-invariance measure and a verdict-agreement matrix are all reported here.")
    r.p("Summary. This chapter presented the building blocks of the system: Python and its libraries, text extraction and temporal reasoning, TF-IDF, BM25 and dense embeddings, the skill ontology with fuzzy matching and evidence-weighted coverage, the concepts of explainability and fairness, multi-agent deliberation and the evaluation metrics. The next chapter specifies the requirements of the system.", indent=False)
